SC BRIEF #207
Friday, September 11, 2026 | The morning read for supply-chain and logistics operators

Good morning. Carriers are reshuffling US port calls, the Northwest had a good August, and warehouse wages keep rising. Four items and the weekend reads.

1. USTR port-fee proposal: carriers restructure services
The proposed per-call fees on vessels built in certain countries have pushed two carriers to reorganize their US services around fewer port calls with larger ships. Fees would phase in over three years starting next April. Smaller ports on the rotations are the likely losers.

2. Northwest Seaport Alliance volumes up 5%
Seattle and Tacoma handled a combined 310,000 TEUs in August, helped by cargo diverted from Vancouver during the summer disruption. The alliance is not counting on keeping it.

3. Warehouse wages up 4%; the automation math shifts
Average hourly warehouse wages rose 4% year over year to $21.40, per Greyfield Warehousing's labor survey. The rule of thumb operators are using: goods-to-person automation pays back in under three years once fully loaded labor cost passes $28 per hour.

4. Weekend reads
A long piece on how the Port of Savannah outgrew its own rail yard; a retrospective on the 2021 container crunch five years on; and a Q&A with a customs attorney on the derivative-steel inclusions process. https://scbrief.io/reads/sept-11

